{"type":"message","id":"26a58657","parentId":"23a3f86e","timestamp":"2026-05-27T07:59:10.086Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_bdrk_01SJn8N16GPyqKP7pVhrpDd7","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"    fn check_unused_fragments_silent_when_fragment_is_used() {\n        let tmp = TempDir::new().unwrap();\n        fs::write(tmp.path().join(\"note.fragment.pan\"), \"# note\\n\").unwrap();\n        let sf = make_source_file(\n            tmp.path(),\n            \"diagnose\",\n            \"---\\nname: diagnose\\ndescription: x\\n---\\n\\n{{> note }}\\n\",\n        );\n        let diags = rules::unused_fragment::check(&[sf], tmp.path(), &SkilletConfig::default());\n        assert!(diags.is_empty());\n    }\n\n    // ── run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    #[test]\n    fn run_returns_true_for_clean_workspace() {\n        let tmp = TempDir::new().unwrap();\n        init_workspace(tmp.path());\n        let skill_src_dir = tmp.path().join(\"src/skills/good\");\n        fs::create_dir_all(&skill_src_dir).unwrap();\n        fs::write(\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779868750086}}
{"type":"message","id":"3ff49e05","parentId":"26a58657","timestamp":"2026-05-27T08:00:08.495Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The test uses `{{> note }}` syntax (double braces), but the parser handles `{> note <}` syntax. So they're different syntaxes! The FRAGMENT_INCLUDE_RE with `{{>...}}` is matching a different syntax than the parser's `{>...<}`.\n\nWait, let me re-read the spec more carefully:\n\n> 2. In `unused_fragment.rs`, replace the `FRAGMENT_INCLUDE_RE` regex with the parser to detect fragment inclusions.\n\nBut the test uses `{{> note }}` syntax and the parser uses `{> ... <}` syntax. These are clearly different!\n\n\nThe spec says to replace the regex, but the tests would break because the existing tests use `{{> note }}` syntax. This seems like a problem. The test content is `\"---\\nname: diagnose\\ndescription: x\\n---\\n\\n{{> note }}\\n\"` and the regex matches `{{> note }}` (Handlebars/Mustache style), while the parser uses `{> name <}` syntax. These are completely different syntaxes, so replacing the regex with the parser would break the existing tests.\n\nLooking at the compiler, `FragmentOpen` is `{>` and `FragmentClose` is `<}`, confirming the parser uses `{> id <}` syntax. The spec says to parse each source file and collect `Node::Fragment` values, but if `Node::Fragment` uses `{>...<}` syntax and the test uses `{{>...}}`, changing to the parser would break the test. I'm checking if `ParsedRefs` already stores fragment data that I should use instead, but looking at the current struct, it only has `typed`, `links`, and `untyped` fields—no fragments field. The spec language about converting supported `Node::Fragment` is vague, and re-reading it, there's a mismatch between what the parser actually matches and what the test expects. but `unused_fragment.rs` isn't actually in the allowed files list, so I shouldn't be modifying it at all. Let me check what files I'm actually permitted to change—it looks like the constraint is stricter than I initially thought.\n\nGiven that limitation, I need to focus on the core changes: adding `var::` and `env::` support to the lexer and parser, exposing the parse module from the compiler's lib, setting up the dependency from skillet to skillet-compiler, and then rewriting `ParsedRefs::extract` to use the parser instead of regex. Let me start implementing these pieces.\n\nFor the `extract` method, I'll map each `Node::Ref` variant to its corresponding `TypedRef` kind, handling the reference, skill, command, variable, environment, agent, and URL cases.\n\nThen I need to handle `Node::RefSuspect` by extracting the source slice, stripping backticks, and classifying it as an untyped reference. For `Node::Fragment`, I'm uncertain whether to include it in `ParsedRefs` for future use by the fragment rule or skip it for now—the plan mentions fragments can be used by the fragment rule, but since `unused_fragment.rs` isn't in the allowed files, I'm not sure if storing them here is the intended approach or if the plan expects the parser to handle that separately. The fragments feature and unused_fragment.rs rule use different syntaxes (Handlebars-style `{{> name }}` vs parser-style `{> name <}`), so leaving unused_fragment.rs unchanged makes sense since it's not in the allowed files. Now I need to plan the actual implementation: adding `RefVar` and `RefEnv` token kinds to the lexer by handling the `'e'` and `'v'` bytes, then adding corresponding `Var` and `Env` variants to `RefKind` in the parser.","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a complete picture. Let me implement all the changes:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01WcmGiwMLrRYY3V3tD18px7","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"X:/repo/skillet/.worktrees/goal-move-the-lint-pipelines-p-mpnrlu69-qmv6ab/crates/skillet-compiler/src/lex.rs","edits":[{"oldText":"    BodyText,\n    Tick,\n    TickDouble,\n    DoubleColon,\n\n    FragmentOpen,\n    FragmentClose,\n\n    RefValue,\n    RefReference,\n    RefSkill,\n    RefAgent,\n    RefCmd,\n    RefPath,\n    RefUrl","newText":"    BodyText,\n    Tick,\n    TickDouble,\n    DoubleColon,\n\n    FragmentOpen,\n    FragmentClose,\n\n    RefValue,\n    RefReference,\n    RefSkill,\n    RefAgent,\n    RefCmd,\n    RefPath,\n    RefUrl,\n    RefVar,\n    RefEnv"},{"oldText":"const AGENT_REF: &[u8] = b\"agent::\";\nconst SKILL_REF: &[u8] = b\"skill::\";\nconst CMD_REF: &[u8] = b\"cmd::\";\nconst REFERENCE_REF: &[u8] = b\"ref::\";\nconst PATH_REF: &[u8] = b\"path::\";\nconst URL_REF: &[u8] = b\"url::\";\n\nimpl<'a> Lexer<'a> {\n    // Returns (kind, keyword_len) where keyword_len excludes the trailing \"::\"\n    fn ref_kind_at(src_bytes: &[u8], start: usize) -> Option<(TokenKind, usize)> {\n        let slice = &src_bytes[start..];\n        if slice.starts_with(REFERENCE_REF) {\n            Some((TokenKind::RefReference, REFERENCE_REF.len() - 2))\n        } else if slice.starts_with(AGENT_REF) {\n            Some((TokenKind::RefAgent, AGENT_REF.len() - 2))\n        } else if slice.starts_with(CMD_REF) {\n            Some((TokenKind::RefCmd, CMD_REF.len() - 2))\n        } else if slice.starts_with(SKILL_REF) {\n            Some((TokenKind::RefSkill, SKILL_REF.len() - 2))\n        } else if slice.starts_with(PATH_REF) {\n            Some((TokenKind::RefPath, PATH_REF.len() - 2))\n        } else if slice.starts_with(URL_REF) {\n            Some((TokenKind::RefUrl, URL_REF.len() - 2))\n        } else {\n            None\n        }\n    }","newText":"const AGENT_REF: &[u8] = b\"agent::\";\nconst SKILL_REF: &[u8] = b\"skill::\";\nconst CMD_REF: &[u8] = b\"cmd::\";\nconst REFERENCE_REF: &[u8] = b\"ref::\";\nconst PATH_REF: &[u8] = b\"path::\";\nconst URL_REF: &[u8] = b\"url::\";\nconst VAR_REF: &[u8] = b\"var::\";\nconst ENV_REF: &[u8] = b\"env::\";\n\nimpl<'a> Lexer<'a> {\n    // Returns (kind, keyword_len) where keyword_len excludes the trailing \"::\"\n    fn ref_kind_at(src_bytes: &[u8], start: u